// std/sysfs.jtr — the filesystem operations that are not ISO C, and the platform's own
// reason when one of them refuses.
//
// TIER: `sys`. The rule, restated because this is the module it was written for:
//
//   **`std` gives portable behavior. `sys` exposes platform-shaped facts.**
//
// `std/fs` answers `bool`. That is the correct answer at its tier — a portable wrapper
// cannot say *why* without inventing a portable reason — and it is not enough to act on.
// "Could not create the directory" is four different problems (it is already there, the
// parent is missing, the volume is full, you may not) and each one is a different next
// move. This module returns the platform's own number, and `std/syserr` turns it into a
// portable category without throwing the number away.
//
// ─── What is here, and what deliberately is not ───────────────────────────────
//
// Here: create/remove a directory, replace-rename, metadata, canonical path, temp
// directory. Every one of them is a thing ISO C cannot do or cannot do the same way
// twice, which is exactly the admission criterion.
//
// NOT here: directory listing (that is `std/sysdir`, which came first), traversal (that
// is `std/walk`, which is `std` because ORDER is a portability decision), and reading or
// writing file contents (ISO C, so `std/file`).
//
// ─── Modification time is NOT in this slice, and the reason is a number ───────
//
// `mtime` is the field a build daemon actually wants, and it is the one field here that
// cannot be had without reading `struct stat` at a hard-coded byte offset. That offset is
// not portable in the way `std/sysdir`'s `D_NAME_OFFSET` is not portable — it is worse:
//
//   st_mtim.tv_sec    Linux x86-64   88     Linux aarch64   88     macOS   48
//   st_mode           Linux x86-64   24     Linux aarch64   16     macOS    4
//
// `D_NAME_OFFSET` is wrong on exactly one platform and is asserted by a test the Linux CI
// job runs. `st_mode` is wrong on an ARCHITECTURE that no CI runner here uses, and a wrong
// `st_mode` reads a `uid` and reports a plausible file type. So the same discipline does
// not transfer, and shipping it would be the untested-branch failure `std/sysdir`'s header
// argues against.
//
// Everything in this module is therefore obtained WITHOUT reading a foreign struct's
// interior. When `mtime` lands it should bring `stat` with it, behind a runtime check
// that validates the offsets against a portable oracle rather than a comment.
//
// ─── The cost `metadata` makes visible instead of hiding ──────────────────────
//
// Windows answers "what kind of thing is this" in ONE call: `GetFileAttributesA` returns
// a bitmask. POSIX has no equivalent that does not go through `struct stat`, so the POSIX
// branch answers it by TRYING: `access` for existence, then an `opendir` that succeeds
// only for a directory, then an `fopen` for the size.
//
// That asymmetry is not papered over, because it is a real cost difference a caller
// walking ten thousand entries needs to know about — up to three syscalls per entry on
// POSIX against one plus an open on Windows. Erasing it behind a uniform `metadata` that
// looked equally cheap on both is exactly the "nice API that erases cost" this tier is
// supposed to refuse.
//
// ─── Two operations answer `bool`, and both answers mean something ────────────
//
//   `make_dir`   → true if THIS CALL created it, false if it was already a directory.
//   `remove_dir` → true if THIS CALL removed it, false if it was already absent.
//
// Both make the operation idempotent, which is what every caller wanted anyway: an
// `EEXIST` on a directory you were trying to create is information, not a failure.
//
// **`rename_replace` answers nothing, and that is the point.** It used to return "did the
// destination exist immediately before" — a REPORT that RACED, since another process could
// create the destination between the probe and the rename, leaving the report stale while
// the rename replaced anyway. It existed only because a fallible Jestyr function was
// required to declare a return type.
//
// It is not any more. `fn f(…) !{ E }` now lowers (`JestyrResult_unit`), so the operation
// says exactly what it does and no more, and the extra `stat` is gone. **A caller that
// genuinely needs to know whether it clobbered something probes for itself** — and then it
// owns the race, visibly, instead of being handed a stale answer by a library that had no
// reason to guess. `sysfs_demo.jtr` does exactly that.
//
// ─── What `rename_replace` actually proves ────────────────────────────────────
//
// Not durability. It proves that the destination NAME refers to the new contents, and
// that no reader ever saw a partially written file under that name. It does NOT prove the
// bytes reached stable storage: neither platform flushes the file's data as a consequence
// of renaming it, and a power cut can leave the name pointing at a file whose blocks were
// never written. Durability needs an `fsync` this module does not yet bind.
//
// It is also not atomic ACROSS FILESYSTEMS. POSIX `rename` fails with `EXDEV` and Windows
// `MoveFileExA` without `MOVEFILE_COPY_ALLOWED` fails too; both surface as the
// `SYSERR_CROSS_DEVICE` category, and the copy fallback is deliberately not taken here
// because a copy is not atomic and pretending otherwise is the exact overclaim this tier
// is meant to avoid.
//
// ─── The capability, and why refusal is a TYPE here and a COUNT in `std/fs` ───
//
// `fs.Fs` is reused rather than a second capability type invented. It is a POLICY value —
// three states over read and mutate — and threading one handle through `std/fs`,
// `std/walk` and this module is worth more than tier purity. `sysfs` importing `std/fs`
// is a dependency on that policy, not on portable behavior.
//
// A refusal here is a typed error (`SysFsRefused`), not a counter bump. `std/fs` counts
// because its operations answer `bool` and a count is the only way to tell "respected the
// capability" from "never tried". These operations answer with an error UNION, so the
// discrimination is in the type, which is strictly better — and `fs.Fs`'s counters are
// private to `std/fs`, so a count here would mean widening that module's surface to
// duplicate information the type already carries. `fs.refused(f)` therefore does NOT move
// when `sysfs` refuses; the test asserts on the error member instead.
//
// Worked example:  examples/std/sysfs_demo.jtr
// Run the tests:   jestyrc test examples/std/sysfs_test.jtr

import "syserr"
import "cstring"
import "sysdir"
import "file"
import "fs"
import "env"
import "str"

// ---- POSIX bindings -----------------------------------------------------------
//
// All five are header-declared, so no prototype is emitted and the platform's own
// declaration — including glibc's versioning and attributes — is what applies.

@cfg(posix) extern "sys/stat.h" fn mkdir(path: cstr, mode: u32) -> i32
@cfg(posix) extern "unistd.h" fn rmdir(path: cstr) -> i32
@cfg(posix) extern "unistd.h" fn access(path: cstr, mode: i32) -> i32
@cfg(posix) extern "stdio.h" fn rename(from: cstr, to: cstr) -> i32
@cfg(posix) extern "stdlib.h" fn realpath(path: cstr, resolved: cptr) -> cptr

// ---- Windows bindings ---------------------------------------------------------

@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn CreateDirectoryA(path: cstr, sa: cptr) -> i32
@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn RemoveDirectoryA(path: cstr) -> i32
@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn MoveFileExA(from: cstr, to: cstr, flags: u32) -> i32
@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn GetFileAttributesA(path: cstr) -> i32
@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn GetFullPathNameA(path: cstr, len: u32, buf: cptr, part: cptr) -> u32

// ---- file kinds ---------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Four, and `SYSFS_FT_OTHER` is load-bearing rather than a fallback for laziness: a FIFO,
// a device node and a socket all exist, are not directories, and cannot be sized. Calling
// them files would make `bytes` a lie for each of them.

pub const SYSFS_FT_MISSING: i32 = 0
pub const SYSFS_FT_FILE: i32 = 1
pub const SYSFS_FT_DIR: i32 = 2
pub const SYSFS_FT_OTHER: i32 = 3

// The longest path either platform will hand back. Linux `PATH_MAX` is 4096 and macOS's
// is 1024, so this is the larger of the two and safe for both; Windows paths without the
// `\\?\` prefix are capped at 260 but `GetFullPathNameA` is given the real buffer size.
const SYSFS_PATH_CAP: usize = 4096

// POSIX `access` mode bit for "does it exist at all".
const SYSFS_F_OK: i32 = 0

// `mkdir`'s mode, 0o777. The process umask masks it down, which is the POSIX convention
// and the reason this is not 0o755: choosing the stricter value here would silently
// override a umask the operator set on purpose.
const SYSFS_MKDIR_MODE: u32 = 511

// Win32. `MOVEFILE_REPLACE_EXISTING | MOVEFILE_WRITE_THROUGH` — the second asks the
// rename itself to reach the disk before returning, which is as close to durable as a
// rename gets on either platform and is still NOT a promise about the file's contents.
// `MOVEFILE_COPY_ALLOWED` is deliberately absent; see the header on cross-filesystem.
const SYSFS_MOVE_REPLACE_WRITETHROUGH: u32 = 9
const SYSFS_FILE_ATTRIBUTE_DIRECTORY: i32 = 16
// `INVALID_FILE_ATTRIBUTES` is `(DWORD)-1`; bound as `i32`, that is -1.
const SYSFS_INVALID_ATTRS: i32 = 0 - 1

// POSIX `EINVAL`, the code reported for a path this module refuses before it reaches the
// platform. Spelled as the platform's own number rather than a private sentinel so
// `syserr.category` classifies it like any other failure instead of needing a special case.
const SYSFS_EINVAL: i32 = 22
// Win32 `ERROR_INVALID_NAME`, the same refusal in the other numbering.
const SYSFS_ERROR_INVALID_NAME: i32 = 123

// What is known about a path, without reading any foreign struct's interior.
//
// `@copy`: an integer and a length. `bytes` is 0 for a directory on BOTH platforms — not
// because they agree (Windows reports 0 and POSIX reports a block count) but because a
// directory's byte size means nothing portable and reporting either platform's answer
// would invite a caller to sum it.
@copy pub struct Meta {
    pub ftype: i32,
    pub bytes: u64,
}

// The value for a path that is not there.
//
// It exists because a module-qualified struct LITERAL does not parse (`sysfs.Meta{ … }`
// from another file is a parse error, though `sysfs.Meta` in type position is fine), so a
// `pub struct` a caller is expected to build needs an exported constructor. Every `catch`
// fallback in a caller wants exactly this value, which is why it is the one provided
// rather than a general `meta(ftype, bytes)`: a caller has no business inventing a size.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn missing() -> Meta {
    return Meta{ ftype: SYSFS_FT_MISSING, bytes: 0 }
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn is_dir(read m: Meta) -> bool {
    return m.ftype == SYSFS_FT_DIR
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn is_file(read m: Meta) -> bool {
    return m.ftype == SYSFS_FT_FILE
}

// ---- shared helpers -----------------------------------------------------------

// The refusal this module reports before touching the platform. An interior NUL truncates
// the path at the C boundary, so `mkdir("a\0/../etc")` would create `a` — a security
// answer wearing a correctness costume, and the same refusal `std/sysdir` and
// `std/file.open_path` make.
@cfg(posix) @no_alloc @no_os fn bad_path_code() -> i32 {
    return SYSFS_EINVAL
}

@cfg(windows) @no_alloc @no_os fn bad_path_code() -> i32 {
    return SYSFS_ERROR_INVALID_NAME
}

// Copy a NUL-terminated C buffer into an owned `String`, bounded by `cap` so a buffer the
// platform did not terminate cannot run off the end.
//
// It COPIES rather than returning a view, and that is not incidental: every caller frees
// the buffer immediately afterwards, so a `str` borrowing it would dangle. The same shape
// as `std/sysdir`'s `name_at`, with the ownership the other direction.
fn owned_from_nul(p: *mut u8, cap: usize) -> String {
    var n: usize = 0
    for n < cap {
        var b: u8 = 0
        unsafe { b = (p + n).* }
        if (b as i32) == 0 { break }
        n = n + 1
    }
    let sl: []u8 = slice(u8, p, n)
    let v: str = from_utf8(sl)
    var s: String = string_new()
    string_push(s, v)
    return s
}

// ---- metadata -----------------------------------------------------------------

// What `path` is, and how big it is if that is a meaningful question.
//
// A missing path is an ERROR rather than `SYSFS_FT_MISSING`, because the platform
// distinguishes "there is nothing there" from "you are not allowed to look" and collapsing
// them would throw away the difference this module exists to preserve. `SYSFS_FT_MISSING`
// is the value a caller stores for an absent entry it already knows about, not an answer
// this function returns.
@cfg(posix) pub fn metadata(mut f: fs.Fs, read a: Allocator, read path: str) -> Meta !{ SysFsFailed(i32), SysFsRefused } {
    if fs.can_read(f) == false { return err(SysFsRefused) }
    if cstring.has_interior_nul(path) { return err(SysFsFailed(bad_path_code())) }

    // Existence first, and the errno is read BEFORE the `CString` is freed. `free` is not
    // required to preserve `errno` on every libc, so anything between the failing call and
    // the read is a chance to lose the reason.
    var c: CString = cstring.from_str(a, path)
    let rc: i32 = access(cstring.as_cstr(c), SYSFS_F_OK)
    var code: i32 = 0
    if rc != 0 { code = syserr.last_raw() }
    cstring.free(c)
    if rc != 0 { return err(SysFsFailed(code)) }

    // Directory? The only portable POSIX test that does not read `struct stat` is to open
    // it as one. `opendir` on a regular file fails with `ENOTDIR`, which is precisely the
    // answer wanted, and the failure is discarded rather than reported: existence is
    // already established, so a failure here means "not a directory" and nothing else.
    var d: sysdir.Dir = sysdir.open(a, path)
    if sysdir.is_open(d) {
        sysdir.close(d)
        return ok(Meta{ ftype: SYSFS_FT_DIR, bytes: 0 })
    }

    return ok(sized_or_other(a, path))
}

@cfg(windows) pub fn metadata(mut f: fs.Fs, read a: Allocator, read path: str) -> Meta !{ SysFsFailed(i32), SysFsRefused } {
    if fs.can_read(f) == false { return err(SysFsRefused) }
    if cstring.has_interior_nul(path) { return err(SysFsFailed(bad_path_code())) }

    // One call answers existence AND kind, which is the whole asymmetry the header names.
    var c: CString = cstring.from_str(a, path)
    let at: i32 = GetFileAttributesA(cstring.as_cstr(c))
    var code: i32 = 0
    if at == SYSFS_INVALID_ATTRS { code = syserr.last_raw() }
    cstring.free(c)
    if at == SYSFS_INVALID_ATTRS { return err(SysFsFailed(code)) }

    if (at & SYSFS_FILE_ATTRIBUTE_DIRECTORY) != 0 {
        return ok(Meta{ ftype: SYSFS_FT_DIR, bytes: 0 })
    }

    return ok(sized_or_other(a, path))
}

// A path already known to exist and not to be a directory: size it if it can be sized.
//
// Unguarded, because it is ISO C on both platforms — `fopen`/`fseek`/`ftell` through
// `std/file`. Something that exists, is not a directory, and cannot be opened for reading
// is a device, a FIFO or a socket, and `SYSFS_FT_OTHER` is the honest answer for all three.
fn sized_or_other(read a: Allocator, read path: str) -> Meta {
    var r: file.Reader = file.open_path(a, path)
    if file.is_open(r) == false {
        return Meta{ ftype: SYSFS_FT_OTHER, bytes: 0 }
    }
    let n: i64 = file.size(r)
    file.close(r)
    // A negative size means `ftell` could not answer — an unseekable stream, which is
    // exactly the `OTHER` case again rather than a file of unknown length.
    if n < 0 {
        return Meta{ ftype: SYSFS_FT_OTHER, bytes: 0 }
    }
    return Meta{ ftype: SYSFS_FT_FILE, bytes: n as u64 }
}

// Does `path` exist at all? The question `metadata` answers with an error, asked in the
// form that does not need one — for the many callers whose next move does not depend on
// why it is absent.
pub fn exists(mut f: fs.Fs, read a: Allocator, read path: str) -> bool {
    let m: Meta = metadata(f, a, path) catch Meta{ ftype: SYSFS_FT_MISSING, bytes: 0 }
    return m.ftype != SYSFS_FT_MISSING
}

// ---- creating and removing directories -----------------------------------------

// Create the directory `path`. **True if this call created it**, false if it was already
// a directory — see the header on why that is the return value.
//
// Only the PARENT's existence is assumed; this is one level, not `mkdir -p`. A recursive
// form belongs above this, in `std`, where the path-splitting decision lives.
@cfg(posix) pub fn make_dir(mut f: fs.Fs, read a: Allocator, read path: str) -> bool !{ SysFsFailed(i32), SysFsRefused } {
    if fs.can_write(f) == false { return err(SysFsRefused) }
    if cstring.has_interior_nul(path) { return err(SysFsFailed(bad_path_code())) }

    var c: CString = cstring.from_str(a, path)
    let rc: i32 = mkdir(cstring.as_cstr(c), SYSFS_MKDIR_MODE)
    var code: i32 = 0
    if rc != 0 { code = syserr.last_raw() }
    cstring.free(c)
    if rc == 0 { return ok(true) }
    return already_a_directory(f, a, path, code)
}

@cfg(windows) pub fn make_dir(mut f: fs.Fs, read a: Allocator, read path: str) -> bool !{ SysFsFailed(i32), SysFsRefused } {
    if fs.can_write(f) == false { return err(SysFsRefused) }
    if cstring.has_interior_nul(path) { return err(SysFsFailed(bad_path_code())) }

    var c: CString = cstring.from_str(a, path)
    // Win32 BOOL: zero is failure. The second argument is a `SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES*`, and
    // null means "inherit the parent's" — the only choice with a portable meaning.
    let rc: i32 = CreateDirectoryA(cstring.as_cstr(c), null)
    var code: i32 = 0
    if rc == 0 { code = syserr.last_raw() }
    cstring.free(c)
    if rc != 0 { return ok(true) }
    return already_a_directory(f, a, path, code)
}

// The shared "it already exists" arm, and it checks rather than assumes.
//
// `EEXIST` / `ERROR_ALREADY_EXISTS` says the NAME is taken; it does not say by what. A
// `make_dir` that reported success because a regular FILE was sitting at the path would
// hand the caller a directory that is not there, and the next write into it would fail
// somewhere far away with a much worse diagnostic. So the name is inspected, and only a
// real directory turns the error into `false`.
fn already_a_directory(mut f: fs.Fs, read a: Allocator, read path: str, code: i32) -> bool !{ SysFsFailed(i32), SysFsRefused } {
    if syserr.category(code, syserr.host_plat()) != SYSERR_EXISTS {
        return err(SysFsFailed(code))
    }
    let m: Meta = metadata(f, a, path)?
    if is_dir(m) { return ok(false) }
    return err(SysFsFailed(code))
}

// Remove the empty directory `path`. **True if this call removed it**, false if it was
// already absent. A non-empty directory is an error, categorized `SYSERR_NOT_EMPTY` on
// both platforms from two different raw numbers (POSIX `ENOTEMPTY`, Win32
// `ERROR_DIR_NOT_EMPTY`) — which is the clearest demonstration in this module of what the
// category layer buys.
//
// It does NOT recurse. Deleting a tree is a traversal, and traversal order is a
// portability decision that belongs to `std/walk`.
@cfg(posix) pub fn remove_dir(mut f: fs.Fs, read a: Allocator, read path: str) -> bool !{ SysFsFailed(i32), SysFsRefused } {
    if fs.can_write(f) == false { return err(SysFsRefused) }
    if cstring.has_interior_nul(path) { return err(SysFsFailed(bad_path_code())) }

    var c: CString = cstring.from_str(a, path)
    let rc: i32 = rmdir(cstring.as_cstr(c))
    var code: i32 = 0
    if rc != 0 { code = syserr.last_raw() }
    cstring.free(c)
    if rc == 0 { return ok(true) }
    return absent_already(code)
}

@cfg(windows) pub fn remove_dir(mut f: fs.Fs, read a: Allocator, read path: str) -> bool !{ SysFsFailed(i32), SysFsRefused } {
    if fs.can_write(f) == false { return err(SysFsRefused) }
    if cstring.has_interior_nul(path) { return err(SysFsFailed(bad_path_code())) }

    var c: CString = cstring.from_str(a, path)
    let rc: i32 = RemoveDirectoryA(cstring.as_cstr(c))
    var code: i32 = 0
    if rc == 0 { code = syserr.last_raw() }
    cstring.free(c)
    if rc != 0 { return ok(true) }
    return absent_already(code)
}

// "Already gone" is success-with-nothing-done; every other reason is a failure.
//
// Unlike `already_a_directory` this needs no second look: `ENOENT` means the name resolves
// to nothing, and there is no kind of thing it could have been instead.
@no_os fn absent_already(code: i32) -> bool !{ SysFsFailed(i32), SysFsRefused } {
    if syserr.category(code, syserr.host_plat()) == SYSERR_NOT_FOUND {
        return ok(false)
    }
    return err(SysFsFailed(code))
}

// ---- rename ---------------------------------------------------------------------

// Move `from` onto `to`, replacing whatever is at `to`. Answers nothing: it either
// happened or it failed, and a caller that needs to know whether something was clobbered
// probes for itself (see the header).
//
// Read the header on what this proves before relying on it: the name flips atomically,
// the bytes are not promised to be on the disk.
@cfg(posix) pub fn rename_replace(mut f: fs.Fs, read a: Allocator, read from: str, read to: str) !{ SysFsFailed(i32), SysFsRefused } {
    if fs.can_write(f) == false { return err(SysFsRefused) }
    if cstring.has_interior_nul(from) { return err(SysFsFailed(bad_path_code())) }
    if cstring.has_interior_nul(to) { return err(SysFsFailed(bad_path_code())) }

    var cf: CString = cstring.from_str(a, from)
    var ct: CString = cstring.from_str(a, to)
    // POSIX `rename` replaces silently and atomically within one filesystem. No flag, and
    // no way to ask it NOT to — which is why the "did it exist" question needs a probe.
    let rc: i32 = rename(cstring.as_cstr(cf), cstring.as_cstr(ct))
    var code: i32 = 0
    if rc != 0 { code = syserr.last_raw() }
    cstring.free(cf)
    cstring.free(ct)
    if rc != 0 { return err(SysFsFailed(code)) }
    // No trailing `return`: reaching the end of a unit-fallible body IS the success path.
}

@cfg(windows) pub fn rename_replace(mut f: fs.Fs, read a: Allocator, read from: str, read to: str) !{ SysFsFailed(i32), SysFsRefused } {
    if fs.can_write(f) == false { return err(SysFsRefused) }
    if cstring.has_interior_nul(from) { return err(SysFsFailed(bad_path_code())) }
    if cstring.has_interior_nul(to) { return err(SysFsFailed(bad_path_code())) }

    var cf: CString = cstring.from_str(a, from)
    var ct: CString = cstring.from_str(a, to)
    // Plain `MoveFileA` REFUSES an existing destination, which is the divergence from
    // POSIX that makes this a `sys` operation rather than an ISO C `rename` call.
    let rc: i32 = MoveFileExA(cstring.as_cstr(cf), cstring.as_cstr(ct), SYSFS_MOVE_REPLACE_WRITETHROUGH)
    var code: i32 = 0
    if rc == 0 { code = syserr.last_raw() }
    cstring.free(cf)
    cstring.free(ct)
    if rc == 0 { return err(SysFsFailed(code)) }
}

// ---- canonical paths --------------------------------------------------------------

// The absolute, lexically normalized form of `path`. The caller owns the result and
// `string_free`s it.
//
// **The two platforms do not compute the same thing, and this is the divergence to know
// about in this module.** POSIX `realpath` resolves every symbolic link along the way;
// Windows `GetFullPathNameA` is purely lexical and resolves none. So on a tree containing
// symlinks the two answers differ, and no amount of wrapping makes them agree — resolving
// links on Windows needs a `GetFinalPathNameByHandleW` on an OPEN handle, which is a
// different operation with different failure modes and different permissions.
//
// What IS made uniform is existence: POSIX `realpath` requires the path to exist and
// Windows does not, so this probes first and both platforms report `SYSERR_NOT_FOUND` for
// an absent path. Leaving that divergence in place would be the worse trap of the two,
// because it makes the same call succeed on one platform and fail on the other for a
// program that never mentions symlinks.
@cfg(posix) pub fn canonical(mut f: fs.Fs, read a: Allocator, read path: str) -> String !{ SysFsFailed(i32), SysFsRefused } {
    if fs.can_read(f) == false { return err(SysFsRefused) }
    if cstring.has_interior_nul(path) { return err(SysFsFailed(bad_path_code())) }

    var buf: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, SYSFS_PATH_CAP)
    var c: CString = cstring.from_str(a, path)
    // The caller-buffer form rather than `realpath(p, NULL)`: the NULL form returns a
    // `malloc`ed pointer that must be handed back to C's `free`, and binding `free` in a
    // tree whose modules already spell `cstring.free` and `free_ptr` is a name collision
    // waiting to be found by someone else.
    let got: cptr = realpath(cstring.as_cstr(c), buf as cptr)
    var code: i32 = 0
    if got == null { code = syserr.last_raw() }
    cstring.free(c)
    if got == null {
        free_ptr(buf)
        return err(SysFsFailed(code))
    }
    var s: String = owned_from_nul(buf, SYSFS_PATH_CAP)
    free_ptr(buf)
    return ok(s)
}

@cfg(windows) pub fn canonical(mut f: fs.Fs, read a: Allocator, read path: str) -> String !{ SysFsFailed(i32), SysFsRefused } {
    if fs.can_read(f) == false { return err(SysFsRefused) }
    if cstring.has_interior_nul(path) { return err(SysFsFailed(bad_path_code())) }

    // `GetFullPathNameA` succeeds on a path that does not exist, so existence is checked
    // first to match POSIX. `metadata`'s error is PROPAGATED unchanged — which is the whole
    // mechanism: an absent path reports `SYSERR_NOT_FOUND` here because it reported it
    // there, rather than because this function invented a second not-found answer.
    //
    // The result is deliberately discarded: `?` is the check. `metadata` never yields
    // `SYSFS_FT_MISSING` (a missing path is an error, not a kind), so a `ftype` test after
    // this would be dead code implying otherwise.
    let m: Meta = metadata(f, a, path)?

    var buf: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, SYSFS_PATH_CAP)
    var c: CString = cstring.from_str(a, path)
    let n: u32 = GetFullPathNameA(cstring.as_cstr(c), SYSFS_PATH_CAP as u32, buf as cptr, null)
    var code: i32 = 0
    if n == 0 { code = syserr.last_raw() }
    cstring.free(c)
    if n == 0 {
        free_ptr(buf)
        return err(SysFsFailed(code))
    }
    var s: String = owned_from_nul(buf, SYSFS_PATH_CAP)
    free_ptr(buf)
    return ok(s)
}

// ---- the temporary directory ------------------------------------------------------

// Where this process may write scratch files. The caller owns the result and
// `string_free`s it. Never empty: both platforms fall back to a fixed location.
//
// **Answered through `env.Env` on BOTH platforms, on purpose.** Windows has
// `GetTempPathA`, and it is not used, because what it does is read `TMP`, then `TEMP`,
// then `USERPROFILE`, then give up on the Windows directory — the same environment
// lookup, performed somewhere a `sealed()` capability cannot see. Going through the
// capability instead means a test can seal the environment and get the documented
// fallback deterministically, and it means the one function in this module that consults
// process configuration says so in its signature.
//
// The trailing separator is NOT included, matching `std/path`'s convention that a
// directory is named without one.
@cfg(posix) pub fn temp_dir(mut e: env.Env) -> String {
    var s: String = string_new()
    let v: str = env.lookup(e, "TMPDIR")
    if v.len > 0 {
        string_push(s, str.strip_suffix(v, "/"))
    } else {
        string_push(s, "/tmp")
    }
    return s
}

@cfg(windows) pub fn temp_dir(mut e: env.Env) -> String {
    var s: String = string_new()
    var v: str = env.lookup(e, "TEMP")
    if v.len == 0 { v = env.lookup(e, "TMP") }
    if v.len > 0 {
        string_push(s, str.strip_suffix(v, "\\"))
    } else {
        // The documented last resort, and it is the one Windows itself falls back to.
        string_push(s, "C:\\Windows\\Temp")
    }
    return s
}
